# 1. PLOTINUS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

plotinus_bio = """<p>Plotinus (c. 204/5–270 CE) stands as the most consequential philosopher of Late Antiquity and the founder of the movement modern scholars call Neoplatonism. Born in Egypt — his precise birthplace is unknown, though Lycopolis (modern Asyut) has been proposed — he studied for eleven years in Alexandria under the enigmatic Ammonius Saccas before joining the abortive Persian expedition of the Emperor Gordian III, which he hoped would give him access to Persian and Indian philosophical traditions. He settled in Rome around 244 CE, where he taught for the remainder of his life under the patronage of the senator Rogatianus and, crucially, of the philosopher and future biographer <a href="../persons/porphyry.html">Porphyry</a>. He died in Campania in 270 CE. His lectures and treatises, composed in Greek and left unedited during his lifetime, were gathered, organized into six groups of nine (<i>Enneads</i>), and published by Porphyry with an attached biography — the <i>Vita Plotini</i> — that remains the principal source for his life. The <i>Enneads</i> constitute the most systematic philosophical account of the metaphysical structure underlying the universe, the soul's relation to that structure, and the conditions for its return to its divine source, produced in the ancient world outside Plato himself. Their influence on Christian, Jewish, Islamic, and Hermetic thought across fifteen centuries is virtually without parallel.</p>

<h2>Intellectual Context and the System of the Enneads</h2>

<p>Plotinus understood himself not as an innovator but as an interpreter of Plato, and the <i>Enneads</i> are formally structured as commentaries on and defenses of Platonic positions. Yet the system he constructed differs in fundamental respects from anything in Plato's dialogues. At its apex stands the One (<i>to hen</i>), an absolutely simple first principle that transcends all predication, all thought, and even Being itself. The One does not deliberate or act: it overflows, as a spring overflows, generating the second hypostasis, Nous (Intellect), by a process of emanation that requires no diminishment of the source. Nous is the realm of self-thinking Thought, the place where Platonic Forms have their reality, and it constitutes the level of Being proper. From Nous in turn proceeds Soul (<i>psyche</i>), the third hypostasis, which is both a unified cosmic soul and the multiplicity of individual souls. The material world — Nature (<i>physis</i>) — is the last and weakest image of the One, produced by Soul's lower activity and characterized by extension, division, and temporality.</p>

<p>This triad of One–Nous–Soul, and the double movement of procession (<i>prohodos</i>) and return (<i>epistrophe</i>), structures everything in the Plotinian universe. The ethical and spiritual task of the individual soul is to retrace the path of emanation inward and upward, progressively withdrawing from sensory attachment, cultivating intellectual contemplation, and ultimately achieving a mystical union (<i>henosis</i>) with the One that is beyond discursive thought. Porphyry reports that Plotinus himself achieved this union four times during their years together — a claim that situates Plotinian philosophy firmly within the tradition of transformative, experience-oriented late antique religion, not merely academic speculation.</p>

<p>Plotinus engaged critically with the Gnostic movements of his day, devoting the long treatise known as "Against the Gnostics" (II.9 in Porphyry's arrangement) to a sustained polemic against what he regarded as their arrogant denigration of the material cosmos, their multiplication of divine intermediaries, and their claim to special revelation bypassing philosophical reason. This polemical engagement is itself evidence of Plotinus's proximity to Gnostic circles in Rome, and modern scholars — including Garth Fowden and the contributors to the <i>Dictionary of Gnosis and Western Esotericism</i> — have noted that the boundary between Plotinian Neoplatonism and what Fowden calls the "philosophical Hermetica" of texts like the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> was permeable and contested rather than rigidly fixed.</p>

<h2>Hermetic Significance</h2>

<p>The relationship between Plotinus and the Hermetic textual tradition is a matter of sustained scholarly debate rather than simple dependence. The philosophical Hermetic texts — particularly the <i>Poimandres</i> (Corpus Hermeticum I), <i>On Rebirth</i> (CH XIII), and the <i>Discourse on the Eighth and Ninth</i> from the Nag Hammadi library — share with the <i>Enneads</i> a set of structural concerns: the soul's heavenly origin and earthly exile, the ascent through planetary spheres, the identification of human Nous with divine Intellect, and the possibility of experiential union with the divine source. Whether the Hermetic texts influenced Plotinus, whether Plotinus influenced the final form of the Hermetic texts, or whether both drew independently on a common Alexandrian philosophical milieu remains unresolved.</p>

<p>What is not in doubt is that the Plotinian conceptual architecture became the primary philosophical framework through which later readers interpreted the Hermetic corpus. When <a href="../persons/marsilio_ficino.html">Marsilio Ficino</a> translated both the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> and the <i>Enneads</i> into Latin in the 1460s–1480s, he read them as complementary expressions of a single ancient theology — the <i><a href="../concepts/prisca_theologia.html">prisca theologia</a></i> — in which Hermes Trismegistus was the Egyptian forerunner of Plato, and Plato's true meaning was explained by Plotinus. This interpretive framework governed Renaissance Hermeticism and has left deep marks on the historiography of Western esotericism down to the twentieth century. The Hanegraaffian correction to this view — emphasizing that the <i>prisca theologia</i> is an early modern construction, not an ancient reality — does not diminish Plotinus's importance; it reframes it. Plotinus matters for the history of Hermeticism not because he "was" a Hermetist but because his philosophical vocabulary became the lens through which Hermetic texts were read, translated, and re-used across the Latin Middle Ages, the Renaissance, and beyond.</p>

<p>The Plotinian doctrine of the Soul's triple aspect also resonated with <a href="../concepts/gnosis.html">gnosis</a> as understood in both Hermetic and Gnostic contexts: the soul's capacity to know itself as divine is simultaneously a cosmological claim (souls originate in Nous) and a soteriological program (that knowledge is itself salvific). This convergence made the <i>Enneads</i> a natural companion text for readers of the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> across the centuries.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Reception</h2>

<p>The transmission of Plotinus was shaped decisively by Porphyry's editorial decisions. By arranging the fifty-four treatises into six groups of nine and prefacing the collection with the <i>Vita Plotini</i>, Porphyry created a canonical text that could be lectured on and commented upon like Plato's dialogues or Aristotle's treatises. <a href="../persons/proclus.html">Proclus</a>, who read and systematized Plotinus extensively in the fifth century, is the principal transmitter of Plotinian Neoplatonism into the Byzantine world and, through his influence on the pseudo-Dionysian corpus, into Latin Christianity. The <i>Theology of Aristotle</i>, an Arabic paraphrase of <i>Enneads</i> IV–VI composed probably in the ninth-century Baghdad circle of al-Kindi, introduced Plotinian ideas into Islamic philosophy under the guise of Aristotelian metaphysics — a crucial and long-undetected act of misattribution that shaped medieval Islamic and, through Latin translations, European scholastic thought.</p>

<p>In the Latin West, direct knowledge of Plotinus was minimal before Ficino's 1492 translation, the <i>Plotini Opera Omnia</i>. But indirect Plotinianism pervaded Augustine, Macrobius, and the Latin Neoplatonic tradition, all of whom absorbed it primarily through Porphyry's intermediary role. The modern scholarly recovery of Plotinus as a figure independent of his Neoplatonic successors was largely the work of nineteenth and twentieth century classical scholarship, culminating in Paul Henry and Hans-Rudolf Schwyzer's critical Greek edition (the <i>editio minor</i>, 1964–1982) and A. H. Armstrong's Loeb translation with commentary (7 vols., 1966–1988).</p>

<h2>Scholarly Debates</h2>

<p>Three major debates surround Plotinus in the context of the study of Western esotericism. First, the question of his relationship to Ammonius Saccas: since Ammonius left no writings, all reconstructions of his teaching depend on inference from Plotinus, Origen the Platonist, and scattered later testimonia — a problem that has generated extensive but inconclusive secondary literature. Second, the question of his dependence on or independence from Gnostic and Hermetic sources: scholars including Christoph Markschies, John D. Turner, and Michael Williams have argued for varying degrees of mutual influence between Plotinian Neoplatonism and Sethian Gnosticism in particular, with the Nag Hammadi texts providing the most direct comparative evidence. Third, the question of the "mysticism" of the <i>Enneads</i>: whether Plotinian <i>henosis</i> is genuinely experiential or is a logical consequence of the system's structure has been debated since Pierre Hadot's influential studies of the 1960s–1980s, with significant consequences for how Plotinus is positioned within the broader history of religious experience.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>

<ul>
<li>Armstrong, A. H., trans. <i>Plotinus: Enneads</i>. 7 vols. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press (Loeb), 1966–1988.</li>
<li>Fowden, Garth. <i>The Egyptian Hermes: A Historical Approach to the Late Pagan Mind</i>. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1986.</li>
<li>Hadot, Pierre. <i>Plotinus, or the Simplicity of Vision</i>. Translated by Michael Chase. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1993.</li>
<li>Henry, Paul, and Hans-Rudolf Schwyzer, eds. <i>Plotini Opera</i>. 3 vols. Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1964–1982.</li>
<li>Porphyry. <i>On the Life of Plotinus and the Arrangement of His Work</i>. Translated by A. H. Armstrong. In <i>Plotinus: Enneads</i>, vol. 1. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press (Loeb), 1966.</li>
<li>Turner, John D., and Ruth Majercik, eds. <i>Gnosticism and Later Platonism: Themes, Figures, and Texts</i>. Atlanta: Society of Biblical Literature, 2000.</li>
<li>Wallis, R. T. <i>Neoplatonism</i>. 2nd ed. London: Duckworth, 1995.</li>
</ul>"""

