# 3. PROCLUS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

proclus_bio = """<p>Proclus Lycaeus, known as the Diadochos ("Successor"), was born in Constantinople in 412 CE and died in Athens in 485 CE, having served for nearly half a century as the head (<i>diadochos</i>) of the Platonic Academy — the oldest continuously operating philosophical institution in the Mediterranean world. He was the most systematic and, in many respects, the most influential philosopher produced by the Neoplatonic movement: more systematic than <a href="../persons/plotinus.html">Plotinus</a>, more comprehensive than <a href="../persons/porphyry.html">Porphyry</a>, and more willing than either to integrate theurgical practice and ritual religion into his philosophical framework, following the lead of Iamblichus. His principal works — the <i>Elements of Theology</i> (<i>Stoicheiosis theologike</i>), the <i>Platonic Theology</i> (<i>Theologia Platonica</i>), the <i>Commentary on the Timaeus</i>, and commentaries on Plato's <i>Parmenides</i>, <i>Alcibiades</i>, <i>Cratylus</i>, and <i>Republic</i> — constitute the most complete philosophical system surviving from antiquity. The range of his influence, extending into Byzantine Christianity through the pseudo-Dionysian corpus, into medieval Islamic and Latin scholasticism through the <i>Liber de causis</i>, and into Renaissance Neoplatonism through direct translation, makes him an indispensable figure in any account of the philosophical substrate that shaped how Hermetic texts were read and interpreted across the post-ancient world.</p>

<h2>Works and Intellectual Context</h2>

<p>The <i>Elements of Theology</i> is Proclus's most formally rigorous work: 211 propositions, each with proof, laying out the structure of reality from the One downward through Henads, Intellect, Soul, and Nature in a manner explicitly modeled on Euclid's <i>Elements</i>. The Henads — a distinctive Proclean innovation — are divine unities that stand between the absolutely transcendent One and the Intelligible realm proper, serving as the philosophical correlates of the traditional Greek gods and allowing Proclus to integrate traditional polytheism into his metaphysical system without reducing it to mere allegory. The <i>Platonic Theology</i> is a longer and less formally structured work that reads the entire Platonic corpus — particularly the dialogues dealing with the Gods, such as the <i>Parmenides</i>, the <i>Philebus</i>, and the <i>Timaeus</i> — as a unified revelation of the divine order. The <i>Commentary on the Timaeus</i>, partially preserved, is one of the most extensive ancient treatments of Platonic cosmology and was a major source for medieval and Renaissance discussions of the world-soul, the demiurge, and the structure of the heavens.</p>

<p>Proclus's triadic dialectic — the movement of remaining (<i>mone</i>), procession (<i>prohodos</i>), and return (<i>epistrophe</i>) — is a systematic elaboration of the Plotinian double movement of emanation and return, formalized into a logical structure that applies at every level of reality. Every being proceeds from its cause, remains partially in that cause, and returns to it through contemplative and, at higher levels, theurgical activity. This triad gave subsequent thinkers — including the author of the pseudo-Dionysian corpus — a formal philosophical vocabulary for describing divine action, creation, and the soul's relation to God that could be adapted to Christian, Islamic, and Jewish monotheistic frameworks with only modest modifications.</p>

<p>In religious practice, Proclus was a committed polytheist and theurgist who composed hymns to the Greek gods, participated in Chaldean theurgical rites, and, according to his biographer Marinus, possessed and exercised supernatural powers. His <i>Commentary on the Republic</i> contains his most extensive treatment of poetry and myth as theological vehicles, arguing against Plato's censure of Homer by insisting on the allegorical depth of mythological narrative — a position that Renaissance mythographers would inherit directly.</p>

<h2>Hermetic Significance</h2>

<p>Proclus does not cite Hermetic texts extensively, and his engagement with the Egyptian priestly tradition was mediated primarily through the Chaldean Oracles and through Iamblichus's <i>De mysteriis</i>. Nevertheless, his importance for the history of Hermeticism is structural and massive. It operates through three channels.</p>

<p>The first is the <i>Liber de causis</i> (<i>Book of Causes</i>). This Arabic text, composed probably in the ninth century CE, draws its propositions almost entirely from Proclus's <i>Elements of Theology</i>, rearranging and condensing them in a way that replaces the Proclean polytheistic framework with a more monotheistic vocabulary. Translated into Latin by Gerard of Cremona around 1167, the <i>Liber de causis</i> was attributed throughout the medieval period to Aristotle and became a standard text in the scholastic curriculum, introducing Neoplatonic metaphysics — the doctrine of the First Cause, of self-subsistent intellects, of the soul as intermediate — into a Christian philosophical tradition that was formally committed to Aristotelian empiricism. Thomas Aquinas, who identified the true source as Proclus after the <i>Elements of Theology</i> became available in William of Moerbeke's Latin translation (1268), wrote a commentary on the <i>Liber de causis</i>. The text's role as a Trojan horse for Neoplatonic metaphysics within scholasticism is directly relevant to understanding how later Renaissance readers could so readily synthesize Aristotle, Plato, Neoplatonism, and Hermeticism — they had been absorbing a covert Neoplatonism throughout their scholastic education.</p>

<p>The second channel is the pseudo-Dionysian corpus. The texts attributed to Dionysius the Areopagite (Paul's Athenian convert, mentioned in Acts 17:34) were composed probably in Syria around 500 CE, and their author drew so heavily on Proclus — including verbatim passages — that several modern scholars have proposed identifying the Pseudo-Dionysius as a member of Proclus's own school. Through the pseudo-Dionysian corpus, Proclean terminology — <i>henosis</i>, the divine darkness, the hierarchical structure of reality from the supreme principle downward through angelic orders — entered Christian mystical theology and liturgical theology as authoritative apostolic doctrine. This gave Neoplatonic concepts a canonical status in both Eastern and Western Christianity that made the subsequent reception of Renaissance Hermeticism far more philosophically legible to Christian readers than it might otherwise have been.</p>

<p>The third channel is direct: <a href="../persons/marsilio_ficino.html">Ficino</a> translated major works of Proclus into Latin alongside the <i>Corpus Hermeticum</i> and Plotinus, reading all three as aspects of a single ancient theology. His <i>In Parmenidem</i> translation (1462) and his translation of the <i>Platonic Theology</i> (1460s) made Proclus directly available to Renaissance Latin readers for the first time and shaped the philosophical framework within which Hermetic texts were interpreted by Pico della Mirandola, Johannes Reuchlin, and the German and Italian Neoplatonists of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. The <a href="../concepts/prisca_theologia.html">prisca theologia</a> lineage as Ficino conceived it — Zoroaster, Hermes Trismegistus, Orpheus, Pythagoras, Plato, Plotinus — implicitly includes Proclus as the most complete ancient philosophical commentator on that lineage, even when he is not named explicitly.</p>

<h2>Transmission and Reception</h2>

<p>In the Byzantine world, Proclus was read and commented upon continuously from his own lifetime through the fall of Constantinople in 1453. Michael Psellus (eleventh century) and later Byzantine Platonists preserved and transmitted the Proclean tradition, linking it with interest in the Chaldean Oracles and with a broader revival of Neoplatonic philosophy within a Christian intellectual culture. The emigration of Byzantine scholars to Italy in the fifteenth century — including George Gemistos Plethon, whose program of Neoplatonic religious revival was explicitly Proclean — brought direct Proclean influence into the Florentine intellectual circle that included Ficino and the early Medici Academy.</p>

<p>In the Arabic and Islamic tradition, Proclus influenced Islamic Neoplatonism through both the <i>Liber de causis</i> and through more direct translations and adaptations. The Ismaili and Sufi philosophical traditions drew on Neoplatonic emanationist metaphysics in ways that parallel the Hermetic synthesis, and the common Proclean source of these parallel developments is an important but still insufficiently explored area of comparative scholarship.</p>

<h2>Literature</h2>

<ul>
<li>Dodds, E. R., ed. and trans. <i>Proclus: The Elements of Theology</i>. 2nd ed. Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1963.</li>
<li>Gersh, Stephen. <i>From Iamblichus to Eriugena: An Investigation of the Prehistory and Evolution of the Pseudo-Dionysian Tradition</i>. Leiden: Brill, 1978.</li>
<li>Morrow, Glenn R., and John M. Dillon, trans. <i>Proclus' Commentary on Plato's Parmenides</i>. Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1987.</li>
<li>Saffrey, H. D., and L. G. Westerink, eds. and trans. <i>Proclus: Théologie Platonicienne</i>. 6 vols. Paris: Les Belles Lettres, 1968–1997.</li>
<li>Steel, Carlos. <i>The Changing Self: A Study on the Soul in Later Neoplatonism — Iamblichus, Damascius and Priscianus</i>. Brussels: Paleis der Academiën, 1978.</li>
<li>Wallis, R. T. <i>Neoplatonism</i>. 2nd ed. London: Duckworth, 1995.</li>
<li>Annas, Julia, and Robin Waterfield. "Proclus." In <i>The Cambridge History of Later Greek and Early Medieval Philosophy</i>, edited by A. H. Armstrong, 302–325. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1967.</li>
</ul>"""

